-- Rollback: Communication: A Biblical Perspective (89aa16b7)
UPDATE public.profiles
SET gospel_data = '[{"title":"Communication: A Biblical Perspective","linkUrl":"https://crosspointechurchmn.snappages.site/media/k5k7y8j/communication","section":"1","subsections":[{"title":"","content":"","questions":[],"nestedSubsections":[{"title":"Miscommunication Problems:","content":"<ol><li><p>Albert Mehrabian states, “A person’s nonverbal behavior has more bearing than his words on communicating feelings or attitudes to others.”<sup>1</sup></p><ol><li><p>Dr. Mehrabian’s formula for importance or weight attached to words, tones, and facial expressions: <br>Total Feelings = 7% words + 38% tones + 55% facial expressions</p></li><li><p>When our words, tones, and facial expressions match, our communication becomes clear, but when the expressions conflict or contradict each other, more weight is placed on facial expressions and tones rather than the actual words we use.</p><ol><li><p>Dr Mehrabian stated, “One of the more common examples of contradictory verbal and nonverbal behavior is sarcasm. ‘How wonderful!’ said with a negative tone of voice is interpreted as ‘How bad!’ because the vocal expression is more important than the words. The same phrase, ‘How wonderful!’ said with a negative vocal expression and a disgruntled frown implies even a stronger negative feeling and is more likely to imply ‘How utterly miserable!’ The negative message in the vocal expression as well as the facial expression accounts for the stronger result.”<sup>2</sup></p></li></ol></li></ol></li></ol>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]},{"title":"Sinful Communication:","content":"<ol><li><p>Sinful communication can harm and destroy relationships, including marriages.</p><ol><li><p>Jeff Forrey states that when couples “communicate in sinful ways the result is often conflict and strained relationships.”<sup>3</sup> <br>Galatians 5:15 But if you bite and devour one another, take care that you are not consumed by one another.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Sinful communication, like all communication, is not limited to how we use our words. We can communicate sinfully with our tones, facial expressions, gestures, and body language.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sinful Communication Defined:</strong> Communicating in ways that violate God’s commands. <br>Ephesians 4:31 Let all bitterness and wrath and anger and clamor and slander be put away from you, along with all malice. <br>Ephesians 4:29 Let no unwholesome word proceed from your mouth, but only such a word as is good for edification according to the need of the moment, so that it will give grace to those who hear.</p><ol><li><p>The word “unwholesome” in this verse means “corrupt”<sup>4</sup> or “unfit for use and worthless.”<sup>5</sup> <br>Colossians 3:9 Do not lie to one another, since you laid aside the old self with its evil practices,</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Sinful communication occurs whenever a person violates any of God’s commands or teachings concerning how we are to communicate with others.</p></li></ol>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]},{"title":"Manifestations of Sinful Communication","content":"<ol><li><p>Anger <br>James 1:19-20 This you know, my beloved brethren. But everyone must be quick to hear, slow to speak and slow to anger; for the anger of man does not achieve the righteousness of God.</p><ol><li><p>Rob Green teaches that “many couples cannot talk about anything significant without ending up in another argument.”<sup>6</sup></p></li><li><p>Anyone who finds themselves conditioned to express sinful anger habitually in any relationship is in danger of “devouring one another” Galatians 5:15.</p></li><li><p>Individuals typically express anger within a relationship by either blowing up or clamming up.<sup>7</sup></p><ol><li><p>Clamming up and blowing up are both sinful ways of expressing anger.</p></li></ol></li></ol></li><li><p><strong>Deception</strong> <br>Ephesians 4:25 Therefore, laying aside falsehood, speak truth each one of you with his neighbor, for we are members of one another.</p><ol><li><p>Husbands and wives sinfully communicate whenever they lie to one another. Their failure to be truthful erodes their trust and ultimately undermines their marriage.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>Additional forms of Sinful Communication.</p><ol><li><p>These include sulking, coarse or foul language, sarcasm, gossip, slander, back-biting, flattery, half-truths, manipulation, clamor, criticism, insults, etc.</p></li><li><p>Any form of sinful communication can be devastating to a marriage. Beevers emphatically states how “Scripture warns us to take our language seriously,” further insisting that “The battle for the tongue is the battle for the Christians life.”<sup>8</sup></p></li><li><p>Brenda Payne says, “Careless or malicious words demonstrate a lack of love toward God and others.”</p></li><li><p>Sinful communication prevents couples from having the oneness that God desires and is a major cause of trouble in marriages.</p></li></ol></li></ol>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]},{"title":"The Importance of Biblical Communication","content":"<ol><li><p>Biblically-based communication is “the basic skill needed to establish and maintain sound relationships.”<sup>9</sup></p></li><li><p>Wayne Mack states that “Wherever you find marital failure, you will find a breakdown in real communication,” however, he continues, “Where you find marital success, you will find a good communication system.”<sup>10</sup></p></li><li><p>Couples must identify and remove any sinful communication patterns and establish new and biblical ways to communicate, relate, and share with one another.</p></li></ol>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]},{"title":"Change Involves the Heart","content":"<ol><li><p>To effectively and permanently change sinful communication patterns, it’s necessary to get to the root of the problem, which is the heart. <br>Matthew 12:34 For the mouth speaks out of that which fills the heart.</p></li><li><p>What is the “Heart?”</p><ol><li><p>Dr. Stuart Scott teaches that the “heart” is the “control center” of man. He further states, “It involves his thoughts, intentions, beliefs, desires, and attitudes. Because all these things really take place in the mind, we can say that the heart could be thought of as the mind. What a man truly wants, thinks, and believes about something is his heart on the matter (no matter if his lips speak differently).”<sup>11</sup></p></li></ol></li><li><p>The Desires of the Heart.</p><ol><li><p>Our desires are of particular importance when it comes to communication. If we desire something too much, we may sin to get it. <br>James 2:14-15 but each one is tempted when, by his own evil desire, he is dragged away and enticed. Then, after desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin; and sin, when it is full-grown, gives birth to death.</p><ol><li><p>Dr. David Powlison says, “Why do you fight? James does NOT say ‘You are fighting because the other person is a blockhead; because your hormones are raging; because a demon of anger took up residence; because humans have an aggression gene hardwired in by our evolutionary history; because your father used to react in the same way; because core needs are not being met; because you woke up on the wrong side of the bed and had a bad day at work.’ Instead, James says, you fight because of ‘your desires that battle within you. You want something but don’t get it.’ The biblical analysis is straightforward and cuts to the core. You fight for one reason: because you don’t get what you want. It does ‘take two to tango.’ So why are you in the dance? You fight because your desire, what pleases or displeases you, what you long for and crave, is frustrated.”<sup>12</sup></p></li></ol></li><li><p>To change sinful communication, we must repent of any evil desires in our hearts and put pleasing God as our main ambition and desire of our heart. <br>2 Corinthians 5:9 Therefore we also have as our ambition, whether at home or absent, to be pleasing to Him. a. When our desire, above all else, is to be pleasing to God from our hearts, sinful communication will begin to cease, and godly communication will prevail.</p></li></ol></li></ol>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]},{"title":"The Four Rules of Communication (A Practical Application to Good Communication)","content":"<p><sup>13</sup></p><ol><li><p><strong>Rule One - Be Honest:</strong> <br>Ephesians 4:25 Therefore, laying aside falsehood, speak truth each one of you with his neighbor, for we are members of one another.</p><ol><li><p>Couples MUST NOT lie to one another Colossians 3:9; they must even guard themselves against exaggerating, embellishing, speaking half-truths, or evading the truth.</p></li><li><p>Individuals bound together in the covenant of marriage must be both sincere and transparently open to one another if they would have a deep relationship; if they would become one as God desires them to be one Genesis 2:24.</p></li><li><p>All communication within a marriage or relationship must always be done in love! Ephesians 4:15 but speaking the truth in love, we are to grow up in all aspects into Him who is the head, even Christ,</p></li></ol></li><li><p><strong>Rule Two - Keep Current:</strong> <br>Ephesians 4:26-27 Be angry, and yet do not sin; do not let the sun go down on your anger, and do not give the devil an opportunity.</p><ol><li><p>When couples resolve to settle problems quickly, they keep their channels of communication open and clear. However, if problems are left unresolved, the result is often anger, which turns into bitterness. <br>Matthew 6:34 So do not worry about tomorrow; for tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.</p><ol><li><p>Jay Adams rightly states, “No one’s shoulders are broad enough to bear the weight of tomorrow’s problems, neither are they able to carry yesterday’s resentments. God wants problems handled one day at a time; and every problem must be dealt with on time.”<sup>14</sup></p></li></ol></li></ol></li><li><p><strong>Rule Three – Attack the Problem, Not the Person: </strong><br>Ephesians 4:29-30 Let no unwholesome word proceed from your mouth, but only such a word as is good for edification according to the need of the moment, so that it will give grace to those who hear. Do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God, by whom you were sealed for the day of redemption.</p><ol><li><p>The Holy Spirit is grieved when Christians use words, tones, and gestures that tear one another down! Therefore, we must put off these unwholesome words, as they are not only of no value, but they are also destructive.</p></li></ol></li><li><p><strong>Rule Four – Act, Don’t React:</strong> <br>Romans 12:21 Do not be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.</p><ol><li><p>Sinful communication and conflicts occur when couples react to one another. To react is to act in opposition to one another; it is to respond sinfully when sinned against.</p><ol><li><p>We will have success only if we are deliberate, intentional, and seek the Lord’s help. <br>Philippians 4:13 I can do all things through Him who strengthens me.</p></li></ol></li><li><p>To act, we must be self-controlled and put off all the sinful types of communication. <br>Ephesians 4:31 Let all bitterness and wrath and anger and clamor and slander be put away from you, along with all malice.</p><ol><li><p>John Stott says, “There is no place for any of these horrid things in the Christian community; they have to be totally rejected.”<sup>15</sup></p></li></ol></li><li><p>Next, we must replace sinful communication patterns with deliberate, intentional, and righteous communication patterns. <br>Ephesians 4:32 Be kind to one another, tenderhearted, forgiving each other, just as God in Christ also has forgiven you.</p><ol><li><p>When we are sinned against, rather than responding in kind, we must actively replace “sinful reactions with godly action” and put on that kindness and tenderness commensurate with living to please God 2 Corinthians 5:9.<sup>16</sup></p></li></ol></li></ol></li></ol><ol><li><p>Albert Mehrabian, Silent Messages, 44.</p></li><li><p>Albert Mehrabian, Silent Messages, 55.</p></li><li><p>Jeffrey S. Forrey, Christian Communication, 37.</p></li><li><p>Jay P. Green, Sr., The Interlinear Bible: Hebrew–Greek–English, 908.</p></li><li><p>George V. Wigram, The New Englishman’s Greek Concordance and Lexicon, ed. Jay P. Green, Sr. (Peabody, MA: Hendrickson, 1982), s.v. “sapro.j.”</p></li><li><p>Rob Green, Communication: The Art of Relationship–Building, 3.</p></li><li><p>Jay E. Adams, The Christian Counselor’s Manual: The Practice of Nouthetic Counseling, 349.</p></li><li><p>Bradley Beevers, “Watch Your Language,” The Journal of Biblical Counseling 12, no. 3 (1994): 24.</p></li><li><p>Jay E. Adams, Christian Living in the Home, 27.</p></li><li><p>Wayne A. Mack, Strengthening Your Marriage, 56.</p></li><li><p>Stuart Scott, The Exemplary Husband, 89.</p></li><li><p>David Powlison, “Getting to the Heart of Conflict: Anger, Part 3,” The Journal of Biblical Counseling 16, no. 1 (1997): 34.</p></li><li><p>Robert Smith, “Four Rules of Communication, Ephesians 4:25-32\" (Lecture presented 11 February 2007 at Faith Baptist Church, Lafayette, IN: lecture notes provided in workbook titled Faith Biblical Counseling: Providing Training and Counseling Based on God’s Sufficient Word).</p></li><li><p>Jay E. Adams, Competent to Counsel: Introduction to Nouthetic Counseling, 223.</p></li><li><p>John R. W. Stott, The Message of Ephesians: God’s New Society, The Bible Speaks Today, 190.</p></li><li><p>Robert Smith, Four Rules of Communication, 3.</p></li></ol><p></p>","questions":[],"scriptureReferences":[]}],"scriptureReferences":[]}],"linkDescription":"Watch the recoding of this content."},{"title":"Homework","section":"2","subsections":[{"title":"Homework Questions","content":"<p>Complete the exercises below and save your answers.</p>","questions":[{"id":"q-1762815699165-cmazjtvcl","question":"<p>Memory Verse: Ephesians 4:29 Let no unwholesome word proceed from your mouth, but only such a word as is good for edification according to the need of the moment, so that it will give grace to those who hear.</p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:01:39.165Z","maxLength":2000},{"id":"q-1762815727530-636ay6o5j","question":"<p>Scripture: Read Ephesians 4:22-32 with your spouse.</p><p>Discuss the Four Rules of Biblical Communication as you go through these verses. </p><ul><li><p>Ask each other, “Where do I typically fail?” Ask each other, “What can I do to communicate more biblically based on these passages?”</p></li></ul><p></p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:02:07.530Z","maxLength":2000},{"id":"q-1762815743329-368lco85v","question":"<p>Review and discuss this lesson with your spouse.</p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:02:23.329Z","maxLength":2000},{"id":"q-1762815771780-km1fv8sxs","question":"<p>Pray daily to communicate biblically, to love your spouse more, and to be a better companion.</p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:02:51.780Z","maxLength":2000},{"id":"q-1762815788546-a7zpmi1z2","question":"<p>Attend Church together.</p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:03:08.546Z","maxLength":2000},{"id":"q-1762815849312-qrorii0ba","question":"<p>Application</p><ul><li><p>Do at least two (2) conference tables this week. Record your results. The Conference Table directions are found on the next page.</p></li></ul><p></p>","createdAt":"2025-11-10T23:04:09.312Z","maxLength":2000}],"nestedSubsections":[]}]},{"title":"The Conference Table","section":"3","subsections":[{"title":"The Conference Table is a tool developed by Jay Adams to help people to learn to both communicate biblically and to resolve problems.","content":"<ol><li><p>Find a quiet place to talk in your home, preferably with a table. Make it a place you do not typically use.</p></li><li><p>Have Ephesians 4:25-32 and the Four Rules of Communication written out and posted, so they are easy to see while you have your conference.</p></li><li><p>Husbands, Read Ephesians 4:25-32 out loud before you begin. Then read the Four Rules of Communication, then lead in prayer.</p></li><li><p>Wives, record the problem(s) you discuss and your biblical solution(s).</p></li><li><p>You must talk for more than 15 minutes but not over 30 minutes.</p></li><li><p>If your spouse breaks any Four Rules of Communication (anger, lying, rolling the eyes, bad tones, etc.), raise your hand, but do not speak.</p></li><li><p>If your spouse raises their hand, you have violated a rule of communication. Do not try to deny it or justify yourself! Believe them!</p></li><li><p>When your spouse raises their hand, you must figure out how you communicated sinfully. After a reasonable period of time, if you cannot figure it out, they can tell you, but it is better if you figure it out yourself!</p></li><li><p>Once you realize how you sinfully communicated, confess your sin to your spouse and ask them specifically to forgive you. Once they have forgiven you, resume the discussion.</p></li><li><p>Use this as an opportunity to concentrate on resolving a problem rather than fighting and learning how you communicate sinfully. You may discover that you were sinning against your spouse in ways you were blind to!</p></li><li><p>When you are finished, husbands, lead in prayer. Thank God for what you each discovered about how you communicate and thank God for any problems you resolved.</p></li><li><p>If your problem wasn’t resolved in 30 minutes, end the session and resume the discussion about this problem at the next conference table. Practice “putting off” sinful communication and “putting on” biblical communication.</p></li></ol><p></p>","nestedSubsections":[],"scriptureReferences":[]}]}]'::jsonb, updated_at = NOW()
WHERE slug = '89aa16b7';
